{
  "abstract": "For n >= 1 let S_n be the mean of the least residues of the squares modulo n and f(n) = sum_{j <= n/4} floor(sqrt(jn)) - (n^2 - 1)/12. Chamberland and Dilcher (arXiv:2510.04387) evaluated f(p) through class numbers and stated five conjectures for composite n. The development proves, for every n >= 1, the reduction of f(n) to S_n and s(n) = #{k < n : n | k^2} in the four classes of n modulo 4 (lattice points under the parabola and the pairing k -> n - k), and the divisor form of the formula for S_n: (n - s(n))/2 - S_n = sum_{d | n} G(d), where G(d) is a sawtooth sum of phi_d = sum_{e | d} mu(d/e) cnt(e, .) and cnt(e, b) counts the square roots of b modulo e. It proves that G vanishes on d = 1, 2 (mod 4), that G(c) = -(1/c) sum_{a<c} a (-c/a) when -c is a fundamental discriminant, and the conductor recursion G(l^2 c) = (l - (-c/l)) G(c) for every prime l and every c > 0 with c = 0, 3 (mod 4); with Dirichlet's class number formula and the conductor formula (quoted) these give G(c) = h*(-c). It derives Conjectures 7.1 (with the sign of one term corrected), 7.2, 7.3 and 7.4 of Chamberland and Dilcher, their formulas (7.3) and (7.4) for n = pq, Bunyakovsky's identity f(p) = 0 for p = 1 (mod 4) and their Proposition 1.2, with the class numbers in Bernoulli form, and Conjecture 7.5 (the limits of f(n)/n) unconditionally, from the bound defect(n)^2 <= n sqrt(n) (1 + log n)^3, proved with the discrete Fourier transform on Z/n, explicit Fourier coefficients of the sawtooth and Weyl differencing for the quadratic Gauss sums (no class numbers). A second proof of Conjecture 7.5 from the class number bound |G(c)| <= C sqrt(c) (1 + log c), stated as a hypothesis, follows the first proof in the note.",
